# `scoot.service`: the compositor inside the user session.
#
# Started by `scoot-session` (the greeter entry command), never enabled
# directly: the launcher waits for IPC readiness, imports
# `WAYLAND_DISPLAY`/`XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP`/`XDG_SESSION_TYPE`, and only then starts
# `scoot-session.target`, which is what pulls in
# `graphical-session.target` -- so units ordered after the session
# target (and `ConditionEnvironment=WAYLAND_DISPLAY` ones, which a
# start before the import would skip without retry) see the display.
# This unit deliberately names no session target in `Wants=` /
# `Requires=` / `BindsTo=`: any of those would pull the session -- and
# with it the graphical target -- in the moment the service forks, long
# before the import, which is the bug this shape fixes. `PartOf=`
# propagates stops only (never starts), so stopping the session target
# stops the compositor, and -- through the session target `BindsTo`
# the graphical target -- stopping the graphical target or logging out
# stops it too. (`Before=graphical-session.target` does not fix the
# old shape: `BindsTo=` starts the listed units regardless of order,
# measured by the sway project against the same target.)
#
# `Type=simple` and no notify: scoot reports readiness over its IPC
# socket (`scoot msg version`), which the launcher polls -- the
# compositor never touches the bus, and there is no `sd_notify` to wait
# on. `Restart=no` (the default, stated): a crash costs every client
# its unsaved state, and silently relaunching into a fresh session
# would hide that; the launcher exits nonzero instead.
#
# The reverse direction -- the compositor stopping taking the session
# down with it -- rides on `OnSuccess=` / `OnFailure=` below: however
# the service leaves the active state (a clean quit, a crash, an
# explicit stop), the shutdown target starts and `Conflicts=` stops the
# session targets, so nothing loops on a missing display. Both lines
# are needed: a clean exit enters `inactive` (`OnSuccess=` only) while
# a crash enters `failed` (`OnFailure=` only). Neither fires on the way
# up -- starting the service enters `activating`, never `inactive` --
# so a normal login can never trip them. Both name units, never binary
# paths the way an `ExecStopPost=` would have to, so the file stays
# portable past NixOS. The launcher starts the same target on exit, so
# the two paths are one teardown, run twice to no harm.
#
# A switch never restarts or stops this unit: `X-RestartIfChanged=false`
# and `X-StopIfChanged=false` tell the NixOS switcher to leave the
# running compositor alone, and the new build takes over at the next
# login. Both live under `[Service]`, never `[Unit]`: the switcher
# reads exactly that section and silently ignores the keys anywhere
# else, the same section its own generated units carry them in. (`X-`
# keys are ignored by systemd itself, so they change nothing at
# runtime.)
#
# Install: the NixOS module (`programs.scoot.session.enable`) writes
# these files with the package binary path filled in. The dependency
# shape follows the session-target pattern systemd documents in
# systemd.special(7) (a session target starts and stops
# `graphical-session.target` with `BindsTo=`; leaf services ride
# `PartOf=`), the shape sway and Hyprland use for their session
# targets (both permissively licensed -- sway MIT, Hyprland
# BSD-3-Clause -- and scoot is MIT: the semantics belong to systemd,
# and the files are written for scoot, not copied).
[Unit]
Description=scoot scrolling-tiling Wayland compositor
PartOf=scoot-session.target
Wants=graphical-session-pre.target
After=graphical-session-pre.target
OnSuccess=scoot-shutdown.target
OnFailure=scoot-shutdown.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=@SCOOT_BIN@ --tty
Slice=session.slice
X-RestartIfChanged=false
X-StopIfChanged=false
